    "result_text": "All tests pass (7 total: the acceptance invariant test + my 6 new unit tests). \n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the business-day rule for the owner registration date so it also skips listed public holidays.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/BusinessDay.java`** \u2014 Extended the existing utility (the single home of this logic) to skip public holidays as well as weekends:\n   - Added a fixed `PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS` set (2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28).\n   - Rewrote `rollForward` to loop forward until it reaches a non-weekend, non-holiday day (via a small private `isBusinessDay` helper). The previous single-jump version could only step over a lone weekend; the loop is required now that consecutive non-business days can chain (e.g. Fri holiday \u2192 Sat \u2192 Sun \u2192 Mon holiday \u2192 Tue).\n\n2. **`ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate.java`** \u2014 Updated the doc comment to state the date rolls past weekends *and* public holidays. No behavior change needed there \u2014 it already delegates to `BusinessDay.rollForward`, so every consumer (daily-capacity count and the stamped entity) picks up the new rule automatically.\n\n3. **`src/test/java/.../util/BusinessDayTest.java`** (new) \u2014 Added focused unit tests covering weekdays (unchanged), weekend roll-forward, a weekday holiday, a holiday landing on a weekend, and the chained Christmas/Boxing-Day sequence \u2014 since holiday skipping couldn't be asserted directly by the existing black-box acceptance test.\n\nThe implementation matches the reference `HOLIDAYS`/`toBusinessDay` loop already present in the acceptance test harness.",
